# Every text file is LF in the index *and* in the working tree, whatever the
# machine's core.autocrlf says. Without this, a Windows checkout under
# autocrlf=true wrote CRLF for whatever git checked out and LF for whatever a
# tool rewrote, so the tree was a mix, git warned on every LF file, and the
# shell scripts broke: bash stops at `$'\r': command not found`, and
# docker/initdb/*.sh runs inside a Linux container that has no autocrlf to
# undo it. .prettierrc's `endOfLine: "lf"` is the same rule for the formatter.
* text=auto eol=lf

# cmd.exe is the one consumer that needs CRLF: with LF-only lines its label
# and `goto`/`call :label` handling misparses near 512-byte boundaries.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

*.png binary
*.ico binary
